Rewritten: what the plugin gave a harness - the five tools of the ego relay
(ego_get_page_text, ego_save_page_markdown, ego_navigate, ego_list_tabs,
ego_status) and the skill that said when to call them - in Common Lisp against
Nodecode's cell contract. The plugin itself carried no code: it was a manifest
naming a loopback MCP server, a Bearer header holding ${EGO_RELAY_TOKEN}, and
that one skill. This cell speaks the same relay directly, JSON-RPC over MCP's
streamable HTTP (initialize, notifications/initialized, tools/call, the
Mcp-Session-Id the relay issues), and answers the same five tools natively:
same tool names, same arguments (url, label, mode, subdir, slug, title), same
answers, with the skill's guidance as (help :ego) and the relay's two reports
also reachable as /ego status and /ego tabs. The bearer token comes from the
section: a `token' secret, or the variable `token_env' names, the same value
the relay was started with. Nothing is embedded and nothing is started: the
relay and the Mac daemon are installed and run separately, as the original
said.

Left out: Hermes's own plugin wiring (the plugin.json config_schema, the
mcp.json server entry and the trust: untrusted approval gate, which is the
host's business rather than the integration's), and the skill's Hermes-specific
sentences - the server name `ego' and the mcp__ego__<tool> spelling, the
v0.23.0 .env interpolation, and the skills_list lookup. Nodecode's own cells
already list, start and stop a cell, so none of that is repeated here. Nothing
was added: no network call of the cell's own beyond the relay the plugin named,
no credential of its own, and no write except the one the save tool asked the
relay for.
